pages.learn=()=>pageHead('A plain-English guide','What is a CRM?<br>Start with your customers.','CRM stands for customer relationship management. It is a way to organise the people your business serves, the conversations you have and the next steps your team needs to take.')+section(`<div class="editorial"><h2>A shared memory<br>for your business.</h2><p>Imagine a customer asks for a quotation on WhatsApp, calls the next day and then speaks to someone else on your team. Without a shared record, the customer may have to explain everything again.</p><p>A CRM gives the team one organised place to find the contact details, relevant notes, enquiry, appointment or sales opportunity. Instead of asking “Who dealt with this?”, you can see what happened, who owns it and what comes next.</p><p>The relationship still belongs to people. The software helps them remember, coordinate and follow through.</p></div>${cardList([['Contact record','The customer’s useful details, company, preferences and notes, kept appropriate to the relationship and your data responsibilities.'],['Conversation history','Context from supported connected channels, so a colleague can understand an enquiry without starting from zero.'],['Pipeline','A simple set of stages showing where an enquiry or opportunity is, such as discovery, proposal and decision.'],['Tasks and next steps','A named owner, a due date and a clear action, such as calling back, sending a quotation or confirming a booking.']])}`)+section(`<div class="section-head"><h2>One enquiry.<br>A connected journey.</h2><p>An illustrative example, not a live customer record.</p></div><div class="learning-steps"><article><h3>1. A customer gets in touch.</h3><p>The enquiry is captured with the customer’s details and source. A colleague can see what the person needs.</p></article><article><h3>2. Someone owns the next step.</h3><p>The team assigns the enquiry and records the useful context, rather than relying on a private chat or memory.</p></article><article><h3>3. The business moves it forward.</h3><p>A booking, discovery call or quotation is linked to the record. A reminder helps the responsible person follow up.</p></article><article><h3>4. The relationship continues.</h3><p>After the visit or sale, the team can plan appropriate follow-up, respect preferences and recognise when the customer returns.</p></article></div>`,'how')+section(`<div class="section-head"><h2>Different businesses.<br>The same need for context.</h2></div>${linkCards([['appointments','APPOINTMENT-DRIVEN BUSINESSES','From enquiry to return visit.','A salon can connect the original question, booking, service preferences and an appropriate rebooking reminder.'],['consultative-sales','CONSULTATIVE SELLING','From discovery to decision.','A B2B supplier can keep requirements, proposal activity, decision-makers and the next follow-up together.']])}`)+section(`<div class="editorial"><h2>CRM, inbox, automation and AI:<br>different jobs, working together.</h2></div>${cardList([['The CRM remembers','It organises customer records, ownership, stages and the next action.'],['The inbox connects','It brings supported conversations into a shared place for your team to respond.'],['Automation follows agreed rules','It can assign a task, send a configured reminder or move information when a defined event occurs.'],['AI helps with suitable work','With approved knowledge and boundaries, it can answer routine questions, gather information and hand off to people. It does not replace responsible decision-making.']])}<div class="editorial"><h3>Why not just use a spreadsheet?</h3><p>A spreadsheet can be a useful starting point. A CRM becomes helpful when several people need a shared history, ownership, reminders and a repeatable process. The goal is not more software; it is a clearer way to work.</p></div>`,'how')+section(`<div class="editorial"><h2>The strategy comes<br>before the setup.</h2><p>Map the steps your customers take. Decide what information is useful at each step, who is responsible and where a simple reminder would help. Then configure the system around that process.</p><p>Start with one useful improvement: putting contacts in one place, agreeing sales stages or making follow-up responsibilities clear. Give the team a small set of everyday habits before adding complexity.</p><h3>A CRM does not fix a process on its own.</h3><p>People still need to keep records current, respect consent and privacy, and act on the next step. Access controls and thoughtful data collection matter. The system supports those responsibilities; it does not remove them.</p><p>AyudaGrow helps with strategy, setup, training and ongoing support within an agreed scope, so the platform can become part of daily work.</p></div>${linkCards([['platform','SEE IT IN CONTEXT','Explore the connected platform.','Try the illustrative conversation, contact, pipeline and workflow views.'],['support','START PRACTICALLY','See how we help.','Turn a clear customer process into a useful working setup.']])}`)+cta();

